\section*{A transcendental-rank obstruction to surface hosts}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Very general cubic fourfolds in sufficiently large admissible Hassett divisors are claimed to be irrational even though their Kuznetsov categories come from ordinary projective K3 surfaces. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 054, 2026-09-24): An additive invariant tests the fixed integral transcendental K3 lattice through a hypothetical birational map. Sarkisov links express its value as endpoint differences on Mori fiber spaces, using vertical-divisor and generic-surface counts; the resulting telescoping constraint contradicts the large-discriminant lattice data.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 054, 2026-09-24): The main theorem supplies a K3 surface host for very general cubics inside certain special Hassett divisors. The problem asks a very general cubic in the entire cubic parameter space; the extra discriminant-locus condition is a genuine restriction.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 054 has no Lean scope page in this snapshot; no formalization of these manuscript results is listed.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

